{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>If you chance upon a bird's nest.</b> These passages are juxtaposed to say that one commandment draws another commandment: through sending the nest he will merit length of days and build a new house and make the commandment of a railing. And what is written after it? ״לא תזרע כרמך כלאים״ — that you may merit a vineyard and sowing. And what is written after it? ״לא תחרוש בשור ובחמור יחדו״ — that you may merit oxen and donkeys and not plow with them together. And what is written after it? ״לא תלבש שעטנז״ — that you may merit a fine garment and make in it ritual fringes. And great is the commandment of ritual fringes, as it concludes in the chapter במה מדליקין: all who are careful in the commandment of ritual fringes merit and are served by two thousand and eight hundred slaves, as it is said: ״ביום ההוא יחזיקו עשרה אנשים״ etc. Another interpretation: ״כי יקרא״ — as it is said: ״קורא דגר ולא ילד עושה עושר ולא במשפט״. What is written after it? ״כסא כבוד מרום מראשון״. What does this have to do with that? To teach you that this bird brings eggs from other birds and sits on them until they emerge from their shells and become chicks, and they grow up and pluck his wings and eat him, and when he wants to flee he cannot, and a wild beast or creeping thing finds him and eats him. What caused this to him? Because he stole eggs that were not his. So too the gentiles who stretched out their hands against the Throne of Glory — for they destroyed His house and burned His sanctuary — in the end their remembrance will be cut off."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:01:19.791209263Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}